<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- SECTION 3: UNDERSTANDING OF THE CLIENT -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="page-break"></div>

<p class="section-tag">Section 3</p>
<h2>Understanding of the Client</h2>

<h3>About the Alberta Equestrian Federation</h3>

<p>The Alberta Equestrian Federation is the province's sole governing body for equestrian sport and industry, recognized by the Government of Alberta since 1978. AEF serves nearly 15,000 members across 425+ Alberta communities, covering everything from competitive sport to recreational trail riding.</p>

<p>Programs include coaching certification, Learn to Ride/Drive, Wild Rose competitions, trail initiatives, and equine welfare advocacy. AEF also operates as the provincial affiliate for Equestrian Canada, connecting Alberta's equestrian community to national programs and standards.</p>

<h3>AEF at a Glance</h3>

<table class="bordered">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="width: 40%;">Detail</th>
<th style="width: 60%;">Information</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Total Members</td>
<td>Nearly 15,000 across Alberta</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Communities Served</td>
<td>425+ across the province</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Newsletter Subscribers</td>
<td>22,000+</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Established</td>
<td>1978</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Programs</td>
<td>Coaching certification, Learn to Ride/Drive, Wild Rose competitions, trail initiatives, equine welfare advocacy</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Insurance Coverage</td>
<td>$5M liability + $40K AD&amp;D with every membership</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>Membership Insurance &amp; Partner Discounts</h3>

<p>Every AEF membership includes $5,000,000 in liability insurance and $40,000 in Accidental Death &amp; Dismemberment coverage, making it one of the highest-value equestrian memberships in the country. This coverage is underwritten and administered through dedicated insurance partners.</p>

<p>In addition, AEF members gain access to exclusive discounts through partner programs, including savings on equine-related products and services through providers like Acera Insurance and Navigate Insurance brokerages. These member-only benefits add tangible financial value beyond the core insurance coverage and serve as a strong incentive for membership sign-ups, particularly for cost-conscious recreational riders and facility operators who carry additional commercial liability.</p>

<div class="callout">
<p style="margin: 0;"><span style="color: #003356; font-weight: bold;">The Goal:</span> Grow total AEF membership by 3% over the next 4 months through a targeted digital marketing campaign. That means reaching the thousands of equestrian participants across Alberta who ride, train, and compete but have not signed up yet.</p>
</div>


<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- SECTION 4: MARKET BARRIERS & OUR APPROACH -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="page-break"></div>

<p class="section-tag">Section 4</p>
<h2>Market Barriers &amp; Our Approach</h2>

<p>AEF does not have a competition problem. It has an awareness and clarity problem. We will fix that.</p>

<h3>The Four Key Barriers to Membership Growth</h3>

<table class="bordered">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="width: 25%;">Barrier</th>
<th style="width: 75%;">Description</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Limited Awareness</td>
<td>Many equestrian participants across Alberta do not know AEF exists or what it does for them. The Federation's digital presence and marketing have not reached a significant portion of the province's active equestrian community.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Unclear Benefits</td>
<td>$5M liability insurance, $40K AD&amp;D coverage, partner discounts, and funding programs are not reaching the right people. The value proposition is strong, but it is not being communicated in the places where potential members spend their time.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>"Not For Me" Perception</td>
<td>Recreational riders, trail riders, and hobbyists often believe AEF is only for competitive equestrians. This misconception excludes the largest potential growth segment from even considering membership.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Transactional Engagement</td>
<td>Current members often renew for insurance alone and do not feel connected to a broader community or movement. This limits word-of-mouth referrals and organic growth.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>Our Approach</h3>

<p>We will reframe AEF membership from a transaction to a statement of identity. When you are part of the equestrian community in Alberta, AEF membership is what responsible participants do. Protection, value, and belonging.</p>

<p>This means shifting the conversation from "you should join" to "this is who we are." Insurance is positioned as a badge of responsibility. Community programs become reasons to stay engaged. And AEF becomes synonymous with being an active, responsible equestrian in this province.</p>


<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- SECTION 5: STRATEGIC APPROACH -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="page-break"></div>

<p class="section-tag">Section 5</p>
<h2>Strategic Approach</h2>

<p>One campaign. Four audience pathways. A unified message adapted for different motivations, all leading to the same outcome: AEF membership.</p>

<h3>Pathway 1: Barn Owners, Facilities &amp; Coaches</h3>
<p style="color: #0072bc; font-size: 10pt; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 2pt;">HIGH PRIORITY / MULTIPLIER AUDIENCE</p>

<p>One facility requiring AEF membership can bring in dozens of riders. This is the highest-leverage audience segment.</p>
<ul>
<li>AEF membership protects your facility, students, and reputation</li>
<li>Professional liability framing, not fear-based</li>
<li>Toolkit assets for facilities to share with riders and boarders</li>
</ul>

<h3>Pathway 2: Broader Equestrian Community</h3>
<p style="color: #0072bc; font-size: 10pt; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 2pt;">RECREATIONAL &amp; NON-COMPETITIVE</p>

<p>Trail riders and hobbyists who do not realize AEF is for everyone.</p>
<ul>
<li>Normalize AEF membership as part of riding in Alberta</li>
<li>Plain-language benefit breakdowns and community stories</li>
<li>"I ride in Alberta. I'm AEF." &mdash; identity-driven messaging</li>
</ul>

<h3>Pathway 3: Families, Youth &amp; New Riders</h3>
<p style="color: #0072bc; font-size: 10pt; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 2pt;">GROWTH SEGMENT</p>

<p>Parents need reassurance. New riders need structure. AEF provides both.</p>
<ul>
<li>Safety-first messaging: insurance as peace of mind</li>
<li>Highlight Learn to Ride/Drive and coaching pathways</li>
<li>Welcoming tone that removes intimidation</li>
</ul>

<h3>Pathway 4: Lapsed &amp; Inactive Members</h3>
<p style="color: #0072bc; font-size: 10pt; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 2pt;">RE-ENGAGEMENT</p>

<p>They said yes once. We remind them why.</p>
<ul>
<li>"Welcome back" email sequences and retargeting</li>
<li>Highlight what is new since they last engaged</li>
<li>Community-driven "you're missed" social proof</li>
</ul>

<h3>How We Talk About Insurance (Without Fear)</h3>

<p>Insurance is the number-one motivator for AEF membership, but fear-based messaging is off the table. We agree with AEF's position on this. Here is how we reframe it:</p>

<table class="bordered">
<thead>
<tr>
<th style="width: 50%; background-color: #991b1b;">What We Will NOT Say</th>
<th style="width: 50%; background-color: #166534;">What We WILL Say</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="background-color: #fff5f5; color: #999999; font-style: italic;">"Without insurance, one accident could cost you everything."</td>
<td style="background-color: #f0fdf4; color: #333333;">"Responsible riders ride protected. AEF membership means $5M in liability coverage, so you can focus on what you love."</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Insurance becomes a badge of responsibility, not a reaction to risk. It is what smart, committed equestrians do.</p>


<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!-- SECTION 6: SCOPE OF SERVICES -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="page-break"></div>

<p class="section-tag">Section 6</p>
